    "title": "unit_test : help text says unknown args are \"ignored\" but they cause EXIT_FAILURE",
    "body": "The help text in `src/unit_test.c` states:\n\n> Unknown arguments are reported but ignored.\n\nHowever, `parse_arg()` returns `-1` when it encounters an unknown key:\n\n```c\n/* Unknown key: report just so typos don't silently pass. */\nfprintf(stderr, \"Unknown argument '-%s=%s'\\n\", key, value);\nreturn -1;\n```\n\nThis propagates through `read_args()` and causes `tf_init()` to return `EXIT_FAILURE`, terminating the program before any tests run.\n\nWindows/MSVC build):\n\n```\n> tests.exe --typo=1\nUnknown argument '-typo=1'\n> echo $LASTEXITCODE\n1\n```\n\nvs. a valid argument, which runs normally:\n\n```\n> tests.exe -i=1\n...\nTotal execution time: 38.234 seconds\n> echo $LASTEXITCODE\n0\n```\n\nGiven the comment in `parse_arg()` (\"report just so typos don't silently pass\"), the fail-fast behavior looks intentional — in which case the fix\nis just updating the help text. But I wanted to check before opening a PR: should the **documentation** be corrected to match the actual\n(fail-fast) behavior, or should the **behavior** be changed to match the documented \"ignored\" claim.",
